From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 11:40:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] input: links are found while an application holds the mouse

An OSC 8 link printed by a full-screen program was a link in a Windows
Terminal cascadia pane and inert text in a ghostty pane on the same
screen - no hover highlight, and a ctrl+click with nothing to open.
Reported from use, with the control run beside it.

ghostty declines to refresh links at all once mouse reporting is on,
unless shift is escaping the capture. Two places carry the same test:
cursorPosCallback, for a pointer move, and keyCallback, for a change of
modifiers. Both are dropped here.

Upstream's rule is coherent on its own terms - the application owns the
mouse, and holding shift is how you escape the grab. It is the wrong rule
for a pane judged beside a cascadia pane, whose `_updateHoveredCell` has
no such test and keeps marking links inside a TUI. And it is wrong in the
worst place: a program drawing a full-screen interface is the most likely
thing to be emitting OSC 8 in the first place, since it is the one that
knows its own text is a link.

keyCallback's other branch - which reset the mouse shape and reported an
empty URL when reporting was on - goes with the gate rather than being
lost. That reset is exactly what a refresh finding no link already does,
in mouseRefreshLinks' trailing `if (over_link)`.

Cost: link detection now runs on cells the pointer enters inside a TUI,
where before it ran on none. That is the same work already done on every
cell outside mouse mode, deduped the same way by mouse.link_point - per
cell, not per pixel - so this is a mode changing rather than a new cost.

zig build test: 3106 passed, 56 skipped, 0 failed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Qm2sT9zLC2AndHGSanf2ws
---
 src/Surface.zig | 75 ++++++++++++++++++++++---------------------------
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 41 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/Surface.zig b/src/Surface.zig
index 06f40b9b8..def45deb9 100644
--- a/src/Surface.zig
+++ b/src/Surface.zig
@@ -2816,39 +2816,24 @@ pub fn keyCallback(
         // Update our modifiers, this will update mouse mods too
         self.modsChanged(event.mods);
 
-        // We only refresh links if
-        // 1. mouse reporting is off
-        // OR
-        // 2. mouse reporting is on and we are not reporting shift to the terminal
-        if (self.io.terminal.flags.mouse_event == .none or
-            (self.mouse.mods.shift and !self.mouseShiftCapture(false)))
-        {
-            // Refresh our link state
-            const pos = self.rt_surface.getCursorPos() catch break :mouse_mods;
-            self.renderer_state.mutex.lockUncancelable(global.io());
-            defer self.renderer_state.mutex.unlock(global.io());
-            self.mouseRefreshLinks(
-                pos,
-                self.posToViewport(pos.x, pos.y),
-                self.mouse.over_link,
-            ) catch |err| {
-                log.warn("failed to refresh links err={}", .{err});
-                break :mouse_mods;
-            };
-        } else if (self.io.terminal.flags.mouse_event != .none and !self.mouse.mods.shift) {
-            // If we have mouse reports on and we don't have shift pressed, we reset state
-            _ = try self.rt_app.performAction(
-                .{ .surface = self },
-                .mouse_shape,
-                self.io.terminal.mouse_shape,
-            );
-            _ = try self.rt_app.performAction(
-                .{ .surface = self },
-                .mouse_over_link,
-                .{ .url = "" },
-            );
-            try self.queueRender();
-        }
+        // Refresh our link state. Not gated on mouse reporting being off -
+        // see cursorPosCallback for why this fork differs there.
+        //
+        // Upstream's other branch, which reset the shape and reported an
+        // empty URL when reporting was on, is gone with the gate rather than
+        // lost: that reset is exactly what a refresh finding no link already
+        // does, in mouseRefreshLinks' `if (over_link)` tail.
+        const pos = self.rt_surface.getCursorPos() catch break :mouse_mods;
+        self.renderer_state.mutex.lockUncancelable(global.io());
+        defer self.renderer_state.mutex.unlock(global.io());
+        self.mouseRefreshLinks(
+            pos,
+            self.posToViewport(pos.x, pos.y),
+            self.mouse.over_link,
+        ) catch |err| {
+            log.warn("failed to refresh links err={}", .{err});
+            break :mouse_mods;
+        };
     }
 
     // Process the cursor state logic. This will update the cursor shape if
@@ -4715,15 +4700,23 @@ pub fn cursorPosCallback(
     // OR
     // 2. the cursor position has changed (either we have no previous state, or the state has
     //    changed)
-    // AND
-    // 1. mouse reporting is off
-    // OR
-    // 2. mouse reporting is on and we are not reporting shift to the terminal
-    if ((over_link or
+    //
+    // Upstream adds a second requirement - that mouse reporting be off, or
+    // that shift be escaping the capture - so that an application holding the
+    // mouse sees no link highlighting at all. This fork drops it. Windows
+    // Terminal's cascadia engine marks a link inside a mouse-grabbing
+    // application too (`ControlCore::_updateHoveredCell` has no such test),
+    // and a ghostty pane beside a cascadia one in the same window has to
+    // agree. Reported from use: an OSC 8 link in a full-screen GUI was a link
+    // in one pane and inert text in the other.
+    //
+    // This costs a mode, not a new cost: it is the same work already done on
+    // every cell the pointer enters when reporting is off. What makes such a
+    // link clickable rather than merely visible is Windows Terminal's own
+    // rule that a ctrl+click outranks a VT mouse report (GH#9396).
+    if (over_link or
         self.mouse.link_point == null or
-        (self.mouse.link_point != null and !self.mouse.link_point.?.eql(pos_vp))) and
-        (self.io.terminal.flags.mouse_event == .none or
-            (self.mouse.mods.shift and !self.mouseShiftCapture(false))))
+        (self.mouse.link_point != null and !self.mouse.link_point.?.eql(pos_vp)))
     {
         // If we were previously over a link, we always update. We do this so that if the text
         // changed underneath us, even if the mouse didn't move, we update the URL hints and state
